{title:Title}

{phang}
{bf:cbsample} {hline 2} C-accelerated bootstrap sampling with replacement


{marker syntax}{...}
{title:Syntax}

{p 8 17 2}
{cmdab:cbsample}
[{it:exp}]
{ifin}
[{cmd:,} {it:options}]

{synoptset 24 tabbed}{...}
{synopthdr}
{synoptline}
{syntab:Main}
{synopt:{it:exp}}positional number of draws within each stratum; default is the original number of sampling units{p_end}
{synopt:{opt cl:uster(varlist)}}variables identifying resampling clusters{p_end}
{synopt:{opt str:ata(varlist)}}variables identifying strata{p_end}
{synopt:{opt id:cluster(newvar)}}create a new cluster ID variable; requires {opt cluster()}{p_end}
{synopt:{opt weight(varname)}}store bootstrap weights instead of resampling{p_end}
{synoptline}
{syntab:Advanced}
{synopt:{opt thr:eads(#)}}maximum number of threads to use{p_end}
{synopt:{opt v:erbose}}display timing breakdown{p_end}
{synoptline}


{marker description}{...}
{title:Description}

{* ctools-credit:begin (generated by scripts/sync_attribution.py; edit config/attribution.json)}{...}
{pstd}
{cmd:cbsample} reimplements Stata's {cmd:bsample} ({cmd:bsample.ado} 2.0.8,
January 17, 2025). See {help cbsample##acknowledgments:Acknowledgments} for
details.
{* ctools-credit:end}{...}

{pstd}
{cmd:cbsample} draws a sample with replacement. A sampling unit is an observation,
or a cluster when {opt cluster()} is specified. By default, the number of draws
in each stratum equals its original number of sampling units.

{pstd}
The sample replaces the data in memory. Selected observations are replicated
according to their draw counts; unselected observations, including those outside
{cmd:if}/{cmd:in} or with missing strata, are dropped. With unequal cluster
sizes, the resulting number of observations can differ from the original.

{pstd}
With {opt weight()}, all observations remain in memory and an existing numeric
variable receives each observation's draw count. Observations excluded from
sampling receive zero.

{pstd}
Use {help set seed} to reproduce a draw with the same data, observation order,
and options. {cmd:cbsample} and {cmd:bsample} need not produce the same sample
from the same seed.

{pstd}
Under {help version} 7 or earlier, {cmd:bsample} uses its old algorithm
({cmd:bsample_7}), so that old scripts reproduce their resamples.
{cmd:cbsample} then calls that program, and the draw, messages and return codes
are {cmd:bsample}'s; {opt threads()} and {opt verbose} are ignored.


{marker options}{...}
{title:Options}

{dlgtab:Main}

{phang}
The expression following {cmd:cbsample} specifies the number of draws within
each stratum. For example, {cmd:cbsample 2, strata(s)} draws two observations
from every stratum; adding {cmd:cluster(c)} draws two clusters from every
stratum. Omitting the count uses each stratum's original number of sampling
units. As in {cmd:bsample}, {it:exp} is an expression, such as {cmd:_N/2},
evaluated in the sample (within each stratum with {opt strata()}, so {cmd:_N}
counts the stratum's observations). It must be constant and an integer
(r(198)), greater than zero and no greater than the available units in any
stratum (r(498)). The size may differ across strata:
{cmd:cbsample _N/2, strata(s)} draws half of each stratum.

{phang}
As in {cmd:bsample}, a cluster sample, or a stratum, with a single cluster is
refused with r(460) ("singleton cluster detected"): its resamples would all be
identical.

{phang}
{opt cluster(varlist)} specifies the variables that identify resampling clusters.
When specified, entire clusters are resampled rather than individual observations.
All observations within a selected cluster receive the same weight.

{phang}
{opt idcluster(newvar)} creates a new variable that identifies each drawn copy
of a cluster, as {cmd:bsample} does: a cluster drawn twice becomes two
clusters, so panel estimators can use the resample ({cmd:xtset} {it:newvar}).
The copies are numbered 1, 2, ... in each stratum, continuing across strata in
stratum order (within each stratum under {help version} 13 or earlier), in
cluster order, or at random when some stratum draws fewer clusters than it
has. The data are sorted by strata, {it:newvar} and the original observation
order. An existing variable of that name is replaced. {opt idcluster()}
requires {opt cluster()} and may not be combined with {opt weight()} (r(198)).

{pstd}
Without {opt weight()}, {cmd:cbsample} drops and duplicates observations in
place, as {cmd:bsample} does, and keeps no copy of the dataset. Break is held
until the sample is complete. An error while the sample is expanded (for
example, not enough memory) leaves the observations dropped or duplicated so
far, and an existing {opt idcluster()} variable is replaced before the draw;
use {helpb preserve} first when the data cannot be reloaded. An expanded
sample requiring an {opt idcluster()} plugin pass must fit within 2^31-1 rows.

{phang}
{opt strata(varlist)} specifies variables identifying strata. Resampling is
performed independently within each stratum. Missing strata are excluded.
Cluster labels reused across strata identify separate sampling units.
{cmd:strata()} and {cmd:cluster()} variables may be {cmd:strL}, with text of
any length and binary values.

{phang}
{opt weight(varname)} overwrites an existing numeric variable with bootstrap
frequency weights. Create it first with {cmd:generate double bsweight = 0}.
When this option is specified, all observations are kept and the weight
variable indicates how many times each observation was selected (0 if not
selected). Storing draw counts uses less memory than creating copies of the
sampled observations.

{dlgtab:Advanced}

{phang}
{opt threads(#)} sets the maximum number of threads, from 1 to 256.
The default follows the {help ctools##threads:ctools thread setting}.

{phang}
{opt verbose} displays progress and elapsed times.


{marker remarks}{...}
{title:Remarks}

{pstd}
The command uses the xoshiro256** generator seeded from Stata's random-number
state. With {opt cluster()}, all eligible observations in a cluster receive
the same draw count. With {opt strata()}, draws are independent across strata.


{marker examples}{...}
{title:Examples}

{pstd}Simple bootstrap sample:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. sysuse auto, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cbsample}{p_end}

{pstd}Bootstrap sample of specific size:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. sysuse auto, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cbsample 50}{p_end}

{pstd}Store bootstrap weights instead of resampling:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. sysuse auto, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. generate double bsweight = 0}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cbsample, weight(bsweight)}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. regress price mpg [fw=bsweight]}{p_end}

{pstd}Stratified bootstrap:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. sysuse auto, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. generate double bsweight = 0}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cbsample, strata(foreign) weight(bsweight)}{p_end}

{pstd}Cluster bootstrap:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. webuse nlswork, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. generate double bsweight = 0}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cbsample, cluster(idcode) weight(bsweight)}{p_end}

{pstd}Reproducible bootstrap:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. sysuse auto, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. set seed 12345}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cbsample}{p_end}


{marker results}{...}
{title:Stored results}

{pstd}
{cmd:cbsample} stores the following in global scalars (accessible via
{cmd:scalar(_cbsample_...)}):

{synoptset 28 tabbed}{...}
{p2col 5 28 32 2: Scalars}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_cbsample_n_selected}}number of distinct observations selected at least once{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_cbsample_total_weight}}sum of draw counts across observations; size of the expanded sample{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_cbsample_nobs}}number of eligible observations{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_cbsample_threads}}number of threads used{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_cbsample_nclusters}}number of clusters (if {opt cluster()} specified){p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_cbsample_nstrata}}number of strata (if {opt strata()} specified){p_end}


